This paper considers a very flexible framework of federated learning where the clients in the distributed system can participate in any inner or outer rounds, asynchronously, and with any number of local iterations.

However, this paper makes a critical error. At the beginning of Section 3.2, the authors said 

$$

abla \Phi(x) =(1/m)\sum_{i\in\mathcal{M}} 
abla f_i(x,y^*(x)) = (1/m)\left\{\sum_{i\in\mathcal{M}} 
abla_x f_i(x,y^*(x))  -   
abla_{xy}^2 g_i(x,y^*(x)) 	imes [ 
abla_{yy}^2 g_i(x,y^*(x)) ]^{-1}  
abla_y f_i(x,y^*(x)) ight\}.
$$

The second equality above is wrong. Note that 
$$
abla f_i(x,y^*(x)) = 
abla_x f_i(x,y^*(x))  -    rac{\partial y^*(x)}{\partial x}  
abla_y f_i(x,y^*(x)) .$$ 

Then by implicit function theorem and the finite-sum structure of $g$, we have 
$$
 rac{\partial y^*(x)}{\partial x} = 
abla_{xy}^2 g(x,y^*(x)) 	imes [ 
abla_{yy}^2 g(x,y^*(x)) ]^{-1} 
= (1/m)\sum_{j\in\mathcal{M}} 
abla_{xy}^2 g_j(x,y^*(x)) 	imes\left [(1/m)\sum_{k\in\mathcal{M}} 
abla_{xy}^2 g_k(x,y^*(x))  
abla_{yy}^2 g_k(x,y^*(x)) ight]^{-1}.
$$
Applying this to the definition of $
abla \Phi(x)$, there exist (three-layer) nested summation over index $i,j,k$, which gives
$$

abla \Phi(x) =(1/m)\sum_{i\in\mathcal{M}} 
abla f_i(x,y^*(x)) = (1/m)\left\{\sum_{i\in\mathcal{M}} 
abla_x f_i(x,y^*(x))  -   (1/m)\sum_{j\in\mathcal{M}} 
abla_{xy}^2 g_j(x,y^*(x)) 	imes\left [(1/m)\sum_{k\in\mathcal{M}} 
abla_{xy}^2 g_k(x,y^*(x))  
abla_{yy}^2 g_k(x,y^*(x)) ight]^{-1} 
abla_y f_i(x,y^*(x)) ight\}.
$$

Compared to what the authors wrote at the beginning of Section 3.2, we can see that the authors simply interchange summation with matrix inversion and ignore the double summation. I originally thought this is just a typo, but when I check the algorithm, I realize the authors really think this is true and develop the algorithm based on that. This can be reflected from Line 15 of Algorithm1, where the authors really just sum up $H_i^{t-	au_i^t}$ which is an approximation of $\sum_{i\in\mathcal{M}} 
abla_x f_i(x,y^*(x))  -   
abla_{xy}^2 g_i(x,y^*(x)) 	imes [ 
abla_{yy}^2 g_i(x,y^*(x)) ]^{-1}  
abla_y f_i(x,y^*(x)) $.

With this big error, I have to recommend rejection.

AC
